## Proposal

### Please re-state the problem that we are trying to solve in this issue.
In chat, typing a short mention that starts with a single character followed by a dot (e.g. `@j.doe`) does not highlight or convert into a recognized mention badge when sent.

---

### What is the root cause of that problem?

There are two layers involved in short mention handling in Expensify (as documented in [`contributingGuides/MENTIONS_HIGHLIGHTING_IN_CHAT.md`](https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/main/contributingGuides/MENTIONS_HIGHLIGHTING_IN_CHAT.md)):
1. **Tokenization Layer (`ExpensiMark` / `expensify-common` & `@expensify/react-native-live-markdown`):**
   - The tokenization regex for `shortMentions` in `ExpensiMark.ts`:
     ```ts
     new RegExp(
         `${Constants.CONST.REG_EXP.PRE_MENTION_TEXT_PART}(@(?=((?=[\\w]+[\\w'#%+-]*(?:\\.[\\w'#%+-]+)*)[\\w\\.'#%+-]{1,64}(?= |_|\\b))(?!([:\\/\\\\]))(?<end>.*))(?!here)\\S{1,254}(?=\\k<end>$))(?!((?:(?!<a).)+)?<\\/a>|[^<]*(<\\/pre>|<\\/code>|<\\/mention-user>|<\\/mention-here>))`,
         'gim'
     )
     ```
   - Running the current shipped `expensify-common@2.0.211` on `@j.doe` produces:
     ```html
     <mention-short>@j.doe</mention-short>
     ```
   - Both upstream PRs ([Expensify/expensify-common#881](https://github.com/Expensify/expensify-common/pull/881) and [Expensify/expensify-common#891](https://github.com/Expensify/expensify-common/pull/891)) are already present. The parser itself tokenizes single-char-dot mentions like `@j.doe` without issues.

2. **Resolution & Styling Layer (`useShortMentionsList.ts` & `ParsingUtils.ts`):**
   - In accordance with Expensify's architecture guidelines:
     > *"Short mention - a special type of mention that contains only the login part of a user email AND the email domain has to be the same as our email domain. Any other `@mention` will not get highlighted if domains don't match."*
   - Specifically, in `useShortMentionsList.ts`:
     ```ts
     const currentUserDomain = getEmailDomain(currentUserLogin);
     if (isDomainPublic(currentUserDomain)) {
         return emptyLoginsList;
     }

     const availableLogins: string[] = [];
     for (const personalDetail of Object.values(personalDetails)) {
         if (!personalDetail?.login || getEmailDomain(personalDetail.login) !== currentUserDomain) {
             continue;
         }
         const [username] = personalDetail.login.split('@');
         if (username) {
             availableLogins.push(username);
         }
     }
     ```
   - And in `ParsingUtils.ts` (`decorateRangesWithShortMentions` & `addDomainToShortMention`):
     ```ts
     if (availableMentions.includes(mentionValue)) {
         return { ...range, type: 'mention-user' };
     }
     ```
     and
     ```ts
     const mentionWithEmailDomain = `${mention}@${userPrivateDomain}`;
     if (availableMentionLogins.includes(mentionWithEmailDomain)) {
         return mentionWithEmailDomain;
     }
     ```

**Why it failed in the reporter's video:**
- In the attached video, the workspace is `Applause's Workspace 16!`.
- The only member invited in the chat history is `zc` (`fdbvdbxzn@gmail.com`).
- There is **no member `j.doe` or `j.doe@applause.expensifail.com` in personal details or workspace members**.
- Because `j.doe` is not an existing user on the sender's private domain, the app intentionally does not format `@j.doe` into a mention badge (preventing arbitrary `@strings` from becoming broken mention links).
- This is the exact identical situation seen in [Issue #96348](https://github.com/Expensify/App/issues/96348) (which had the identical regression test case ID `https://github.com/Expensify/Expensify/issues/557782`), where the issue was confirmed **not reproducible / invalid test prerequisites** and closed.

---

### What changes do you think we should make in order to solve the problem?

#### Primary Recommendation (QA / Test Verification):
Verify with proper test prerequisites:
1. Ensure the logged-in user is on a private domain (e.g. `user1@testdomain.com`).
2. Invite a member whose login has a single character followed by a dot on the **same private domain** (e.g. `j.doe@testdomain.com`).
3. Type `@j.doe` in the workspace chat.
4. **Result:** The mention `@j.doe` is highlighted in the composer via `decorateRangesWithShortMentions` and sends as `<mention-user>@j.doe@testdomain.com</mention-user>` as expected.

#### Alternative (If Product Team wants short mentions to resolve across all workspace members regardless of domain matching):
If product design decides that short mentions within workspace chats should resolve for any workspace member even across different or public domains, we should update `useShortMentionsList.ts` and `ReportUtils.ts` to look up member logins from `report.participants` or `policy.employeeList` instead of strictly enforcing `currentUserDomain === memberDomain`:

In `src/hooks/useShortMentionsList.ts`:
```diff
--- a/src/hooks/useShortMentionsList.ts
+++ b/src/hooks/useShortMentionsList.ts
@@ -28,9 +28,6 @@ const buildAvailableLoginsList = memoize(
         if (!personalDetails) {
             return emptyLoginsList;
         }

         const currentUserDomain = getEmailDomain(currentUserLogin);
-        if (isDomainPublic(currentUserDomain)) {
-            return emptyLoginsList;
-        }

         const availableLogins: string[] = [];
         for (const personalDetail of Object.values(personalDetails)) {
-            if (!personalDetail?.login || getEmailDomain(personalDetail.login) !== currentUserDomain) {
+            if (!personalDetail?.login) {
                 continue;
             }

             const [username] = personalDetail.login.split('@');
             if (username) {
                 availableLogins.push(username);
             }
         }
         return availableLogins;
```

---

### What alternative solutions did you explore? (Optional)
Investigated whether autocomplete suggestion handling in `SuggestionMention.tsx` had an issue with dotted handles; confirmed that [PR #81262](https://github.com/Expensify/App/pull/81262) already added `normalizedPrefix` handling for trailing dots, so typing `@j.` or `@j.doe` matches any existing member whose handle starts with `j.` without issues.

